A 1% yield gain needs z = 14,000, $ln K approx 9.8 times 10^7$. The class has at most about $2^66$ isomorphism classes, so it cannot supply it. Even the full subspace family (heuristically independent, which it is not) gives 5e-5. Selection also costs more than it returns: evaluating $p_E$ of one candidate means enumerating $2^44$ points of V, and moving the instance to another class member needs a verified isogeny chain, both charged per candidate under the 0, 0.1 and 1 GAE band (§5a) with no effect on the sign of the conclusion: the relative yield gain is below $10^(-4)$ and the dimension saved is below $10^(-4)$ bit while the price is at least $2^44$ field operations per candidate. Only a factor base that is not a subspace (F chosen as E-type points, $p_E = 1$ by construction) escapes this, and it is the relabelling of the counting-identity section paid for by losing the linear structure the 2^(l+1)-eliminations method needs. That is a different lead (non-subspace bases), not this one.

== Scope and limits

- The data are toy fields (n <= 19, l <= 11, 40 groups, five curves per n), one seed, `A_random` targets drawn uniformly from E(F_q) minus O and T. $|G|$ comes from the census for 4 of the 40 groups (n = 7, 11, 13 only) and is approximated by $2^n + 1$ for the other 36, i.e. 18 distinct curves (n = 11: 4, n = 13: 4, n = 17: 5, n = 19: 5, including the Koblitz curve at n = 17 and 19). The approximation moves predictions by up to about 3% at n = 11 and below 0.6% at n >= 17.
- The identity is exact only for the first moment over R, and for the unconditional mean over all R in G. The clean sample excludes R in S, O and T; to O(1/|G|) its expectation is $binom(abs(S) + 1, 3) slash abs(G)$ at m = 3, i.e. the stated prediction times $2 v_E slash (2 v_E + 3)$ (0.954, 0.977, 0.988, 0.994 for n = 11, 13, 17, 19). The data cannot tell the two apart (cell SEs 1 to 2%), so the "0.97 to 1.02 agreement" is a few-percent check; the slope tests are unaffected. The m = 2 slope SE of 0.19 is conservative (a target-level bootstrap gives about 0.13, z against 2 about 1.5), the slope test has no pre-stated acceptance rule (it is post hoc on data the twist study had already seen), and the expected slope under the identity is about 3 - 6/|S|, indistinguishable from 3 here. It predicts nothing about the cost of the Groebner or elimination step per probe, which the presentation-invariance ICC studies (n = 31 and 23, m = 3) measured separately and found null.
- The large-l statements (section "What choosing b...") are heuristic extrapolations of $sigma_p$; the adversarial ceilings (factor 2 in dimension, 8 in yield) are unconditional but unreachable by any known construction.
- No ECDLP cost was measured. No row for the IC leaderboard or `benchmarks/`; no matched rho is involved, so §5a pricing is stated but not exercised: no priced table exists, and the sentence in section 3 that the 0, 0.1 and 1 GAE band does not change the sign rests only on 2^44 point tests against a gain below $10^(-4)$. The n = 131 (m = 83 / 31) numbers are pure extrapolation from toy fields with n <= 19 (n prime; ord_n(2) = 10, 12, 8, 18 against 130); the closure is not established at the target size, and no ECDLP claim is made.
- Unchecked: whether the 5.8% floor/crater yield deficit and 7.2% model effect recorded in LEAD-STR (prime fields, l = 2 volcanoes) are $(p_"floor" slash p_"crater")^m$ at unmatched |F|. The identity predicts they are if |F| differs; the recorded |F| per curve was not examined here.

== Atlas disposition (not applied; closure pending review)

This note adds evidence. It does not close anything. A closure claim goes to the breakthrough siblings (validator-breakthrough, red-team-breakthrough) in their own sessions (AGENTS.md "Model tiers"); this note is not that review.

- *LEAD-STR-class-representative-ic-selection*: suggested wording is "evidence added; closure of the sub-claim 'pick the isomorphic or isogenous model with the best small-x factor base' pending breakthrough review and the |F| check". The scope that this note supports is narrow: binary subspace bases, first-moment yield, under the random-character-sum heuristic for n = 131 (validated only at l <= 11). It does not cover S (only the first moment), the prime-field volcano evidence of the lead (this note's data are binary toy fields, n <= 19), or the transport and floor/crater arms. The 5.8% floor/crater and 7.2% model effects have not been checked for being |F| relabelling; that free check (compare the recorded |F| of the floor and crater arms; if the yield ratio is explained by $(|F|$ ratio$)^m$, match the preregistered three-arm study on |F|) should be run first and decides the disposition. The unconditional ceiling that remains is a factor 2 in cost.
